y their own staff which  would obviously make hr complaints  easier if it's your direct line manager  that is you know the issue  i have no complaints personally about  that i think as long as mps are able to  select their interview and select the  right staff members for them i would  also say the most important thing this  is it just just just to come in if you  don't mind um because you you're  obviously uh upset by the story i get it  you know you you've come on and very  strongly explained why you find it  troubling but what do you think should  change i understand that you're going to  give me an answer about the conservative  party but surely we should be looking at  things that can practically change in  parliament to try and stop this kind of  behavior or at least make a bit of a  difference but for me it comes down to  are mps going to be held accountable for  their actions are they going to have the  right standard of leadership set from  the topic is there going to be you know  some of the people were talking those  are good words but there's no actual  change there is there  wouldn't be change if people took this  seriously and therefore made decisions  accordingly if you have a situation  where someone has resigned for a certain  set of behaviors and you then appoint  them not just to the whips office but to  be in charge of the welfare of other mps  well of course that's the wrong decision  that's what needs to change and that  just clearly hasn't been a factor in the  prime minister's considerations here at  all okay um  there's just to move on to some other uh  issues now there's chaos at the airports  at the minute anyone trying to book a  holiday will be very well aware of it  the government's come forward with some  plans today where they want to  fast-track track security checks for  baggage handlers to try and ease the  staffing issues does labor support that  or are you worried about any security  risk well i want to see the department  of transport get a grip on this travel  chaos i saw that story last night to be  frank i think they've probably just come  up with that to try and get through the  media round today there seems to me to  have been no planning at the stage of  the pandemic that required it to look at  what would happen when we came out of  the pandemic in terms of the job losses  that had taken place  at airports in particular about the  complex relationship between the home  office employees the airline staff and  the airport staff to make sure we had  the capacity to do it so it feels like  they're playing a little bit of catch up  today but they do need to get a grip of  it uh okay um and then just finally uh  tony blair said this week on a podcast  that he supports what kirsten was doing  but he then went on to say the central  problem is what are the ideas that are  going to stop this relegation and put  the uk on an upward path there's been a  gaping great hole where ideas should be  do you agree that there's been a gaping  great hole where idea should be  no i think this has been a gap in great  hall but i think tony blair's analysis  of what this country needs in terms of  having a plan to turn around the last  decade of poor growth poor productivity  downward pressure on wages is absolutely  essential i was doing a big speech this  week at the um society of motor  manufacturers summit about how the state  could be a partner to make sure we've  got the battery factories that we need  in order to make sure we continue to  make electric vehicles in this country  aren't and don't lose out to competitors  and that is what is required if you look  at the conservative party this  government what are they offering you  bring back imperial measurements a trade  war with the european union that is not  the way forward if you look at what  we're saying and of course we've got to  develop this it's that exciting  long-term vision for industrial policy  in this country to make sure there are  